A tiny IRC client for the ZX Spectrum
=====================================
irc.c is an IRC client built on top of this stack, in the same style as
tcp_chat.c: the bottom line of the screen is what you type on and the 23
lines above it are the log.

The full IRC protocol is far too much for a 48k Spectrum that talks to
the world through a bit banged serial port, so this implements only the
handful of messages you need to join a channel and talk on it. Anything
it does not understand is simply printed as it arrived, and /raw lets you
send any command the server understands even if the client does not.

Building and running
====================
  . /data/sahlberg/z88dk/zcc-setup.sh
  make irc.bin

which also produces irc.tap. Then, as for every other program here:

  ./NET.sh                                    # console 1, needs sudo
  fuse --rs232-tx=/tmp/tx --rs232-rx=/tmp/rx  # console 2

and load irc.tap in FUSE. NET.sh is what sets up the forwarding and the
masquerading, without it the Spectrum can only reach 192.0.2.1.


Starting up
===========
The program asks four questions before it connects:

  IRC server IP:            a naked IP address, see below
  Port [6667]:              empty means 6667
  Nick:                     your nick, at most 16 characters
  Channel (empty for none): joined automatically once registered

Then it connects, sends NICK and USER, and drops into the chat screen.
Type /help to get the command list on the screen.

CAPS SHIFT + SYMBOL SHIFT + C quits (it sends a QUIT first). CAPS SHIFT
+ 0, or CAPS SHIFT + 5, is backspace. Do not expect the keyboard to be
fast: the program is inside the serial port nearly all of the time and
only looks at the keyboard about 40 times a second.


There is no name resolution
===========================
This stack has no DNS, no resolver and no hosts file, so the server has
to be given as a naked IP address. Look it up on the Linux host first:

  $ host irc.libera.chat
  irc.libera.chat has address 93.158.237.2
  irc.libera.chat has address 130.185.232.126
  ...

Note that the round robin above is exactly what DNS is there for. The
addresses change, servers get rotated out, and the one you wrote down
last month may well be gone today. If a connect fails, resolve the name
again before assuming the program is broken.

The same goes for the other direction: several networks look up the
reverse DNS of the address you connect from and some of them refuse
connections that have none. Since we come out of slip-tun's masquerading
we appear as the Linux host, so that is normally fine.


Commands
========
Anything that does not start with a / is said on the channel you are
currently on.

  /join #channel     join a channel, and make it the current one
                     (/j is a synonym)
  /part [reason]     leave the current channel
  /nick name         change your nick
  /msg nick text     send a private message (/m is a synonym)
  /me action         send a CTCP ACTION, i.e. "* nick waves"
  /names [#channel]  list who is on the channel
  /topic [text]      show the topic, or set it if text is given
  /raw command       send a raw IRC command, e.g. "/raw WHOIS bob"
                     (/quote is a synonym)
  /quit [reason]     disconnect and exit
  /help              print the list above

The commands are case insensitive, so /JOIN works as well as /join.
Everything else, /list, /whois, /away, /mode, /invite, /kick, ... is
still available through /raw, you just have to read the reply as plain
text. Do not use "/raw LIST" on a large network, it will send you tens
of thousands of lines that the Spectrum has no chance of keeping up
with.


What you see
============
Messages are formatted like most other clients do:

  <bob> hello there          something said on a channel
  *bob* hello there          a private message sent to you
  >bob< hello there          a private message you sent
  -bob- hello there          a notice
  * bob waves                a /me
  * bob joined #zx           join, part, quit, nick, kick, topic, mode
  *** connected. ...         messages from the client itself

Numeric replies from the server (the welcome, the MOTD, the name list,
all the error replies) are printed as plain text with your own nick
stripped off the front. Anything the client does not know about is
printed as the command name followed by its parameters.

Only printable ASCII is ever put on the screen. That is not only for
looks: the console driver treats several control codes as "take the next
byte or two as my arguments", so a stray byte off the network could
otherwise move the cursor or change the colours.


Servers to test with
====================
1. Your own, on the Linux host. This is by far the best way to test.
   ngircd is small, needs no configuration to speak of and is packaged
   everywhere:

     $ sudo dnf install ngircd          # or apt install ngircd
     $ ngircd -n                        # -n = foreground, ^C to stop

   The packaged /etc/ngircd/ngircd.conf is good enough as it is. It
   listens on port 6667 on all addresses, so from the Spectrum
   connect to 192.0.2.1 (that is slip-tun's end of the link, i.e. the
   Linux host). Join the same channel with a normal client to talk to
   yourself:

     $ irssi -c 127.0.0.1 -p 6667

   Everything stays on the machine, so you get the whole protocol at
   full speed with no rate limits and nobody to annoy while you debug.

2. ncat, if you want to see the protocol by hand. Run

     $ ncat -l 6667

   and connect the Spectrum to 192.0.2.1. You will see the NICK and USER
   lines arrive, and whatever you type in the ncat window is fed to the
   client as if it came from a server. Try typing

     :bob!bob@example.com PRIVMSG #zx :hello spectrum
     PING :test

   Remember that the client only acts on a line once the newline has
   arrived, and that it needs the 001 welcome numeric before it will
   auto join the channel you asked for at startup:

     :fake 001 yournick :Welcome

3. A public network. These work, but they are not the place to debug.
   Plaintext IRC on port 6667 is still offered by, among others,
   Libera.Chat, OFTC, EFnet, IRCnet and Undernet. Resolve the name on
   the host with "host <name>" as shown above and give the client the
   address you get:

     irc.libera.chat   irc.oftc.net   irc.efnet.org
     open.ircnet.net   irc.undernet.org

   Libera.Chat has a channel dedicated to retro and vintage computing,
   which is the obvious place to try this client out. See the next
   section for how to get there.

   Things to expect out there:

   - No TLS. This client speaks plaintext only, so port 6697 and any
     network that has turned plaintext off is out of reach. Assume
     everything you type is public.
   - Connecting takes a while. Many servers do a reverse DNS lookup and
     an ident lookup first, and the ident one only gives up after ten
     seconds or so. The screen just sits there until the welcome
     arrives.
   - The MOTD is long. A hundred lines of text arriving at a machine
     that has no serial receive buffer means a lot of retransmissions,
     and it can take a while to settle down. That is normal, see the
     note about the missing receive buffer in README.
   - Registration may need more than we send. Some networks want SASL
     or a server password before they let you in, and this client has
     neither, though "/raw PASS ..." right after connecting can work.
   - Flood protection. Sending several lines in quick succession can
     get you disconnected with "Excess flood". The Spectrum is not
     really capable of typing that fast.
   - Channels are busy. Join something quiet like a channel of your own
     rather than a channel with a thousand people in it, or the client
     will spend all of its time drawing text.


Chatting on #occlub, the retro channel on Libera.Chat
=====================================================
#occlub on Libera.Chat is dedicated to real time retro and vintage
computing discussion, which makes it about the friendliest audience
there is for a Spectrum that has just talked its way onto the internet
through a serial port. You do not need a registered nick to join, and
Libera.Chat still accepts plaintext connections, which this client
needs since it cannot do TLS.

  irc.libera.chat   plaintext on 6665-6667 and 8000-8002
                    TLS on 6697, 7000 and 7070 (no use to us)

1. Look the name up on the Linux host, since the Spectrum has no
   resolver:

     $ host irc.libera.chat
     irc.libera.chat has address 93.158.237.2
     irc.libera.chat has address 130.185.232.126
     irc.libera.chat has address 108.181.132.149

   Any one of them will do. It is a round robin, the addresses do
   change, and the ones above are only what it answered when this was
   written, so look it up yourself rather than copying them.

2. Start the bridge and the emulator as usual:

     $ ./NET.sh                                    # console 1, sudo
     $ fuse --rs232-tx=/tmp/tx --rs232-rx=/tmp/rx  # console 2

3. Load irc.tap and answer the four questions:

     IRC server IP:            93.158.237.2  (whatever host printed)
     Port [6667]:              press ENTER, 6667 is the default
     Nick:                     what you want to be called, e.g. speccy48
     Channel (empty for none): #occlub

4. Now wait, and do not panic when nothing happens. A public server
   takes its time before it lets you in:

   - First a few notices scroll by, "*** Looking up your hostname" and
     the like.
   - Then nothing at all for ten seconds or so, while the server tries
     an ident lookup that we never answer and eventually gives up on.
   - Then the welcome (numeric 001) and the MOTD, which is a lot of
     text for a machine with no serial receive buffer. It arrives
     slowly and in fits and starts. That is expected.
   - When that is over the client joins #occlub by itself, and you get
     the topic and the list of who is there.

5. Now just type, and press ENTER to send. Anything that does not start
   with a / is said on the channel, and your own line is echoed back as
   "<yournick> ...". The handful of commands worth knowing:

     /names             who is on the channel right now
     /me waves          sends "* yournick waves"
     /msg nick hello    a private message to one person
     /topic             show what the channel is about
     /quit see you      leave (CAPS SHIFT + SYM SHIFT + C does the same)

6. If somebody already has the nick you picked, the server refuses it
   and you stay unregistered until you choose another one:

     /nick speccy48b

What to expect from the channel itself, if you have not used IRC before:

* Silence is normal. IRC is not a messaging app. People stay connected
  all day and answer when they come back to the keyboard, so a question
  can sit there for hours before somebody replies. An empty looking
  channel is not a broken connection, and /names will show you that
  there are plenty of people in it.
* Say what you are connecting from. A 48k Spectrum on a bit banged
  serial line running a home made TCP stack is exactly the sort of
  thing that channel exists for, and it explains in advance why you
  type slowly and drop off now and then.
* Everything is in the clear. Plaintext IRC means the SLIP link, the
  Linux host and every hop after it can read what you type. Never type
  a password into this client. If you want to register your nick with
  NickServ, do it from a normal client over TLS on the host, not from
  the Spectrum.
* Expect to be dropped occasionally. When that happens the client says
  so and exits, and you just load it again. Reconnecting is completely
  normal on IRC and nobody will think anything of it.


Limitations
===========
* One connection, one channel at a time. /join simply replaces the
  current channel, it does not close the old one, so a JOIN that the
  server refuses leaves you talking into a channel you are not on.
* Lines from the server longer than 320 characters lose their tail.
  Lines you type are limited to 100 characters.
* No CTCP replies. VERSION, PING and friends sent to you are shown as
  text and not answered.
* No TLS, no SASL, no DCC, no colours, no logging, no scrollback and no
  nick completion.
* The client is polite about the one thing that matters, PING, and
  ignores nearly everything else.


Testing without a Spectrum
==========================
  make test

builds and runs test/test_irc, which mocks the z88dk console driver and
#includes irc.c so that the real code is tested: message formatting,
line reassembly across segments, PING handling, the commands and the
screen handling. It does not exercise any networking, which still needs
FUSE and slip-tun.
